# =============================================================================
# d64 — integer ↦ d64 takes the error channel
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • integer → decimal — `n ↦ d64` scales the integer to the carrier, but
#     the carrier has a range: 5 widens to `5`, while 92233720369 is out of
#     range for the d64 carrier and the conversio fails into the function's
#     own error channel (`⇥ string`), so the handled call prints `caught`.
#   • The `do { } catch err { }` handler around the calls is what lets the
#     conversio failure recover; the channel is part of the function type,
#     so every call must sit inside a handler.
#
# The unhandled side is deliberately NOT taught here: a widen call outside a
# channel handler traps (nonzero exit), and that trap is already pinned by
# the Rust golden test
# `decimal_golden_test::integer_to_d64_conversio_takes_the_error_channel_and_still_traps_outside`
# in crates/radix-hir-go. A trapping exemplum would need separate
# exit-code plumbing, so this exemplum pins only the handled path.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • expecting `92233720369` to widen — the scaled value does not fit the
#     d64 carrier, and the failure goes to the channel, not to the value
#
# See also: ↦, ⇥, int, d64
# =============================================================================

# decimal — integer ↦ d64 through the error channel
#
# WHY: pins the error-channel ruling for the out-of-range integer
# conversio end-to-end at the runner: the widening failure takes the
# enclosing channel instead of trapping when the call is handled.
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   conversio_channel.expected — `5` then `caught`.

fn widen(int n) → d64 ⇥ string {
    return n ↦ d64
}

main {
    do {
        print widen(5)
        print widen(92233720369)
    }
    catch err {
        print "caught"
    }
}
